#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""pr-wait: ONE waiter per PR head for CI and Codex's review.

  pr-wait PR [--repo OWNER/NAME] [--head SHA] [--since ISO] [--out PREFIX] [--timeout S]
             [--interval S] [--codex-stuck S] [--ci-grace S] [--no-codex] [--no-request]
             [--rerequest-after S] [--json]

Why (2026-10-23): three waiters per head (CI, Codex, adversary) were two thirds of the
notification turns of a 579-turn lead transcript, and the load watcher found eight duplicate
waiters on 2026-10-20. This is the one aggregating waiter: it wakes the lead once, when
  1. every CI check on the exact head SHA is finished (`check-runs` plus commit statuses of
     that SHA through `gh api`, not the PR's current checks), and
  2. Codex's review of that exact head is terminal (AGENTS.md code review rule 9): the comment
     carrying `<!-- codex-pull-request-review-summary -->` holds `<!-- codex-review-state:v1
     {...} -->`, edited in place; `code_review.commit_sha` must equal the head and
     `code_review.status` be `completed` or `failed` (both terminal; a marker for another sha
     never matches).
Then it prints ONE line and writes, each atomically (temp file beside it, then os.replace):
  PREFIX-checks.json   the normalized checks (name, kind, state, conclusion) of the head
  PREFIX-codex.json    the state marker of the head (the newest marker seen when none matches; {} none)
  PREFIX-findings.json Codex's inline findings for the head
                       (`null` when not collected: --no-codex, or the wait ended before Codex did)
  PREFIX-open-findings.json  every unresolved Codex review thread of any head (see Open findings)
  PREFIX-summary.json  the one-line result as JSON
PREFIX defaults to ~/.local/state/pr-wait/<owner>__<name>-pr<N>-<head12>. Output files are written on
every end except exit 2 and 4 (nothing was waited for).
Both conditions are re-judged on every poll (a check suite registered late, a re-run, a re-requested
review); it finishes only when both hold on the same poll.

Only the Codex app is believed: a state marker counts only from a comment whose author is
`chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]` (type Bot) and whose `performed_via_github_app.slug` is
`chatgpt-codex-connector`; the repo is public, so anyone can post a comment holding the markers.

Findings for the head are the PR's inline comments (`pulls/N/comments`) that carry
a finding mark (`<!-- codex-code-review-finding:v1 -->` or `<!-- codex-security-review-finding:v1 -->`), are authored by that bot, and belong to the head: their
`original_commit_id` is the head, or their `pull_request_review_id` is a `pulls/N/reviews` entry whose
`commit_id` is the head. No time window (2026-10-23, adversary round 1: Codex's own receipt comments
quote `@codex review`, so a "newest request" moved past real findings; PR 12 on 2026-10-20). GitHub
re-anchors `commit_id` of a finding on an unchanged line, but not these two. `--since ISO` is an optional
extra lower bound on created_at.

Open findings: a finding of an EARLIER head stays open until its review thread is resolved (PR 32,
2026-10-23: Codex left a P1 on head 3c40470, two pushes followed, the marker for the new head said
`has_unresolved_findings: true` and the head-only filter said 0). So the waiter also reads the PR's review
threads (`gh api graphql`, `reviewThreads.isResolved`, the authority) and lists every unresolved Codex
thread of any head, outdated or not, in PREFIX-open-findings.json (`null` when not collected); a thread
counts when ANY of its comments (up to 100) is a Codex finding. Fixing a finding means fixing the code,
then resolving its thread.
Two Codex reviews, treated alike: code findings carry `<!-- codex-code-review-finding:v1 -->` and the
marker's `code_review`; security findings carry `<!-- codex-security-review-finding:v1 -->` and the
marker's `security_review` (PR 20 thread 4361214081 is an open one). Every finding and open thread has a
`kind` (`code` or `security`). The wait ends only when `security_review` is terminal too, if the marker
carries one. A marker WITHOUT `security_review` is not blocked on it (nothing to verify; the open threads
of both kinds still gate) and the summary says `security_status: absent`.
Summary fields: has_any_findings and has_unresolved_findings (marker `code_review`) with security_ twins,
security_status, findings_for_head (and _by_kind), open_findings (unresolved Codex threads, outdated or
not; open_findings_by_kind) and open_findings_outdated (the outdated subset), findings_mismatch (list of
reasons; per kind the marker and the threads disagree: has_unresolved_findings true with no open thread of
that kind, false with some, or has_any_findings true with no Codex finding comment of that kind; never
print a clean 0 over one), merge_ready and merge_blockers. merge_ready is true only when: at least one CI
check succeeded and none failed or is pending (skipped and neutral count as passed beside a success,
never alone), Codex's code review completed on this head, the security review too when the marker carries
it, zero open Codex threads of either kind, no mismatch, and the marker states has_unresolved_findings as
a boolean for each review it carries. merge_blockers lists why not: codex-<status>, security-<status>
(security-failed, security-running, security-other-head), findings-not-collected, marker-unverified,
security-marker-unverified, no-checks, ci-pending, ci-failed, ci-none-succeeded, open-findings,
findings-mismatch, codex-skipped. The exit code does not carry it: the caller reads merge_ready.

Requesting the review (2026-10-24; operator: "Make pr-wait request the review itself"). The Codex bot
reviews on PR open and draft-to-ready, NOT on a push: a pushed head needs a comment `@codex review`
(PR 55: 45 min of "codex-stuck" because nobody had asked). So when the poll finds NO Codex marker for the
head and NO request since the head was pushed, pr-wait posts ONE comment `@codex review` (`gh api -X POST
repos/R/issues/N/comments -f body=...`, an argv list). It never posts when a marker for the head exists
(any status), with `--no-codex`, or with `--no-request`.
"A request since the push" is decided by SERVER-STAMPED times, never by a commit's own date and never by
timeline position (adversary round 1, 2026-10-24: GitHub places a `committed` timeline item by the commit's
own, author-controlled date, so a commit made before a request but pushed after it sits BEFORE that request
and the request, made for the previous head, would count for the new one). The push time of the head is the
EARLIEST `created_at` of its check suites (`gh api --paginate repos/R/commits/H/check-suites`; GitHub makes
the suite when the push arrives); for a force-push also the `created_at` of the LATEST `head_ref_force_pushed`
item of the PR timeline (`gh api --paginate repos/R/issues/N/timeline`) whose `commit_id` is the head; the
LATEST of the stamps that exist. `pushedDate` (GraphQL) is null in practice (checked on PR 55, 2026-10-24).
A request is a `commented` timeline item whose body, stripped, is exactly `@codex review`, whose author is
the PR author, the repo owner (the OWNER of --repo) or the authenticated `gh` user (`gh api user`), and
whose server `created_at` is LATER than the push time (parsed UTC datetimes, never strings). Codex's own
receipts and anyone else's quotes of the phrase never count (AGENTS.md rule 9: PR 12 and PR 10). With NO
server push stamp (no CI app on the repo) no request can be dated: it leans toward posting (zero counted);
the cap of two POST ATTEMPTS per head (an errored POST counts, and is saved BEFORE it is sent) and the
own-request state file bound the duplicates. If a gh call fails (check
suites, timeline, user) the case is undecidable: nothing is posted, `codex_request_note` says so, and the
next poll retries (re-reading both before any post). At most TWO requests per head: if the marker for the
head is still non-terminal (`running`) `--rerequest-after` seconds (default 600) after pr-wait's own request,
it re-requests ONCE more (only when the timeline shows fewer than two requests and pr-wait has made fewer than
two POST attempts: a POST that errored spends one, and then `codex_request_note` says the re-request was
skipped); then `codex_request_note`
points to openai/codex#55890 (a manual request sometimes stays "Running") and the usual `--codex-stuck`
bound ends the wait. The delay is pr-wait's own wall clock (a wait, not an ordering decision). The count of
pr-wait's own requests, of its POST attempts, the time of the last request, a pending attempt and the ids of the
timeline comments already counted as its own persist in
`request-<repo>-<pr>-<head>.json` in $PR_WAIT_STATE_DIR (repo lower-cased; written atomically, read with `.get`,
`own` and `attempts` clamped to 0..2, `adopted_ids` at most 8 ints, a non-finite time dropped): a second pr-wait
on the same head, started after the first exited, never posts again past that count. A POST that errored may
still have landed: a request by an allowed author, body exactly `@codex review`, not an id already counted,
whose server `created_at` is between the attempt time less 120 s of clock skew and the attempt time plus 240 s
(a later one is a person's) AND, when a push stamp exists, later than it (floor = max(push, attempt - skew): a
request for the previous head never counts), is recorded ONCE as pr-wait's own, so its re-request is not lost
and no second post follows it. With a push stamp, below the cap, a request since the push that the own count does
not explain adopts as well. With NO push stamp a request for the previous head made within 120 s before the
attempt is indistinguishable from the landed POST: it is adopted, and the post for the new head is missed (a
stall bounded by `--codex-stuck`; never an extra post). A state write that fails is a
warning in `codex_request_note`, never a crash. The summary gains `codex_requested` (0, 1 or 2: the requests
pr-wait posted for the head, all runs) and `codex_request_note`.

A `gh` failure (or empty `gh` output) is "unknown", never "done": it is retried on the next poll and
reported in the summary (`errors`) when the wait ends. A PR that does not exist (HTTP 404) is a usage error.

Codex stuck: no terminal marker for the head `--codex-stuck` seconds (default 2700) after the wait
started (marker absent) or after the marker was first seen non-terminal (running) ends the wait with
result `codex-stuck`, never a silent timeout. CI with no checks at all counts as pending for
`--ci-grace` seconds (default 120) after the wait started, because a fresh push registers its check runs
a little later; after that, zero checks is "no checks" and finished.

Exit: 0 done (CI finished and Codex terminal, whatever each said: read the files and `merge_ready`),
      1 timed out, 2 bad usage, no such PR, or the lock takeover guard held by a live pid for 30 s,
      3 Codex stuck, 4 another pr-wait for the same
      repo + PR + head is alive (its pid and output prefix on stderr: never re-arm it),
      5 the PR head moved (a new push; the new head is in the line and the summary),
      6 timed out while `gh` was failing (the last poll did not get through).
Duplicate guard: a lock file `lock-<repo>-<pr>-<head>` in $PR_WAIT_STATE_DIR (default
~/.local/state/pr-wait), created atomically with its content (temp file, then os.link), holding the pid
and a process start identity (Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat starttime, immune to wall-clock steps; macOS:
`ps -o lstart=`); a dead pid, or a live pid whose identity differs (pid reuse), is stale and taken over
under a takeover guard that holds its taker's pid and start and is broken by liveness.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import atexit
import json
import math
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

DEFAULT_REPO = "djbclark/djbclark-ade"
SUMMARY_MARK = "<!-- codex-pull-request-review-summary -->"
STATE_RE = re.compile(r"codex-review-state:v1\s+")
FINDING_MARKS = (("codex-code-review-finding:v1", "code"), ("codex-security-review-finding:v1", "security"))
KINDS = ("code", "security")
CODEX_LOGIN = "chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]"
CODEX_APP = "chatgpt-codex-connector"
CODEX_GQL_LOGIN = "chatgpt-codex-connector"  # GraphQL author login: no [bot] suffix
TERMINAL = ("completed", "failed")
PASS_CONCLUSIONS = ("success", "neutral", "skipped")
GH_TIMEOUT = 120
REQUEST_BODY = "@codex review"
STUCK_ISSUE = "openai/codex#55890"
MAX_REQUESTS = 2  # `@codex review` POST attempts per head, an errored one counted
CLOCK_SKEW = 120  # seconds the local clock may differ from GitHub's when matching an attempt to a comment
MAX_IDS = 8  # timeline comment ids kept in the request state file (two requests per head; a few spare)

EXIT_DONE, EXIT_TIMEOUT, EXIT_USAGE, EXIT_STUCK, EXIT_DUP, EXIT_MOVED, EXIT_GH = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6


class GhError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, msg: str, not_found: bool = False) -> None:
        super().__init__(msg)
        self.not_found = not_found


# ---- gh -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def gh(*args: str) -> str:
    """Run `gh` with an argv list; any failure is a GhError, never an empty success."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["gh", *args], check=False, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=GH_TIMEOUT
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise GhError(f"gh {args[0]} {args[1] if len(args) > 1 else ''}: {e}") from e
    if p.returncode != 0:
        err = (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip().replace("\n", " ")[:300]
        raise GhError(f"gh {' '.join(args[:2])}: rc={p.returncode} {err}", "HTTP 404" in err)
    return p.stdout


def parse_pages(text: str) -> list:
    """`gh api --paginate` prints one JSON document PER PAGE, concatenated: decode them all."""
    dec = json.JSONDecoder()
    docs, i, n = [], 0, len(text)
    while True:
        while i < n and text[i].isspace():
            i += 1
        if i >= n:
            return docs
        try:
            doc, i = dec.raw_decode(text, i)
        except ValueError as e:
            raise GhError(f"unparsable gh output at byte {i}: {e}") from e
        docs.append(doc)


def api_items(endpoint: str, key: str | None = None) -> list:
    """All items of a paginated list endpoint; `key` names the array inside an object page."""
    items: list = []
    docs = parse_pages(gh("api", "--paginate", endpoint))
    if not docs:
        raise GhError(f"{endpoint}: empty gh output")
    for doc in docs:
        if key is not None:
            part = doc.get(key) if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
        else:
            part = doc
        if isinstance(part, list):
            items.extend(part)
        else:
            raise GhError(f"{endpoint}: unexpected page shape {type(doc).__name__}")
    return items


def pr_doc(repo: str, pr: int) -> dict:
    """The `pulls/{pr}` document. ONE call, and it is the only place that endpoint is read.

    The adversary verdict comes out of the same document (its `body` and `labels`), so reading it
    again would double the calls to this endpoint - which matters because the test harness serves
    routes by CALL COUNT, and because an extra round trip per poll is waste.
    """
    try:
        return json.loads(gh("api", f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}"))
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
        raise GhError(f"pulls/{pr}: unexpected shape: {e}") from e


# ---- CI -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---- adversary verdict ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The adversary's verdict is read from a MACHINE-READABLE MARKER in the PR body, never from prose:
#
#     <!-- adversary-verdict:v1 {"verdict":"APPROVE"|"BLOCK","head":"<40-hex>"} -->
#
# WHY A MARKER AND NOT PROSE (2026-10-25, after two adversary rounds):
# The first version parsed English. Round 1 found six false APPROVEs and five false MISSES. It was
# patched and 14 tests added; round 2 found fourteen NEW false APPROVEs, including a BLOCK silently
# deleted whenever its reason contained "never", "must not", or named another reviewer. The lesson is
# structural, not a missing case: **the artefact under test was a claim about English**, and no set of
# heuristics makes that correct, because correctness would mean "no sentence anyone could write defeats
# it" - an unbounded set. A filter must only ever DOWNGRADE; the prose version could delete a BLOCK.
#
# THE PRECEDENT IS THE HOUSE'S OWN: AGENTS.md rule 9 reads Codex's review state from
# `<!-- codex-review-state:v1 {...} -->` and matches the `:v1` SUFFIX deliberately (a waiter whose regex
# lacked it saw no state on PR 12 and ran its full 30 minutes). Same shape here, on purpose.
#
# THE PROPERTIES, each of which a test covers:
#   1. `:v1` is part of the match. A marker without it is not this marker.
#   2. A missing or unknown `verdict` value is `adversary-missing` - never a default. Fail closed.
#   3. `head` must be 40 hex AND equal the PR head. Another head is `adversary-other-head`, not a pass.
#   4. A malformed marker is `adversary-malformed`, its own outcome, so a typo reads as a typo rather
#      than as an absence.
#   5. NEVER fall back to prose. No marker means "no machine-readable verdict", full stop; a fallback
#      would reintroduce the parser through the back door and the failure would be invisible again.
#   6. A BLOCK can never be downgraded by anything - no ordering, no other field, no warning.
# The marker is matched LOOSELY (anything up to the closing `-->`) and then validated, so a marker
# with broken JSON is reported as `malformed` rather than as an absence. A strict `{...}` regex cannot
# see a malformed marker at all, which would make a typo indistinguishable from "no verdict".
MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"<!--\s*adversary-verdict:v1\s*(.*?)\s*-->", re.DOTALL)
# A marker START with no closing `-->` is invisible to MARKER_RE, and GitHub renders an unclosed
# `<!--` as a comment to the end of the body, so a human does not see it either. Count the starts and
# compare with the matches: any excess is malformed (adversary review, A2).
MARKER_START_RE = re.compile(r"<!--\s*adversary-verdict:", re.IGNORECASE)
HEX40 = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{40}$", re.IGNORECASE)


def _strict_object(pairs):
    """`object_pairs_hook` that rejects a DUPLICATE key.

    Without it, `{"verdict":"BLOCK","head":H,"verdict":"APPROVE"}` is read last-wins, so a BLOCK is
    silently downgraded by a second key (adversary review, A3).
    """
    seen = set()
    for k, _ in pairs:
        if k in seen:
            raise ValueError(f"duplicate key {k!r}")
        seen.add(k)
    return dict(pairs)




def adversary_verdict(body: str, head: str) -> dict:
    """Read the adversary verdict marker from a PR body and decide whether it names this head.

    Returns {"present", "verdict", "quoted_sha", "matches_head", "conflict", "malformed", "detail"}.
    Never inspects prose: a body full of verdict-shaped sentences and no marker is simply
    `present: False`, which is the point.
    """
    if not body:
        return {"present": False, "verdict": None, "quoted_sha": None, "matches_head": False,
                "conflict": False, "malformed": False, "detail": "no PR body"}

    matches = MARKER_RE.findall(body)
    starts = len(MARKER_START_RE.findall(body))
    if not matches:
        if starts:
            # A marker START with no `-->`: malformed, NOT absent.
            return {"present": False, "verdict": None, "quoted_sha": None, "matches_head": False,
                    "conflict": False, "malformed": True,
                    "detail": f"{starts} adversary-verdict marker(s) with no closing `-->` "
                              f"(an unclosed `<!--` runs to the end of the body)"}
        return {"present": False, "verdict": None, "quoted_sha": None, "matches_head": False,
                "conflict": False, "malformed": False,
                "detail": "no adversary-verdict marker in the PR body (prose verdicts are not read)"}

    # Validate EVERY marker, so a malformed one beside a good one is still reported.
    parsed: list[dict] = []
    problems: list[str] = []
    if starts > len(matches):
        problems.append(f"{starts - len(matches)} marker(s) with no closing `-->`")
    for raw in matches:
        try:
            doc = json.loads(raw, object_pairs_hook=_strict_object)
        except ValueError as e:
            problems.append(f"marker is not valid JSON: {e}")
            continue
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            problems.append("marker is not a JSON object")
            continue
        verdict = doc.get("verdict")
        sha = doc.get("head")
        # EXACT match: the spec says "APPROVE"|"BLOCK". A lowercase or odd-cased value is outside it
        # and fails closed (adversary review, A5).
        if verdict not in ("APPROVE", "BLOCK"):
            problems.append(f"verdict must be exactly APPROVE or BLOCK, got {verdict!r}")
            continue
        if not isinstance(sha, str) or not HEX40.match(sha):
            problems.append(f"head must be a 40-hex sha, got {sha!r}")
            continue
        parsed.append({"verdict": verdict, "head": sha.lower()})

    if not parsed:
        # A marker was present but unusable: its OWN outcome, so a typo is visibly a typo.
        return {"present": False, "verdict": None, "quoted_sha": None, "matches_head": False,
                "conflict": False, "malformed": True,
                "detail": "adversary-verdict marker is malformed: " + "; ".join(problems)}

    for_marker = [p for p in parsed if p["head"] == head.lower()]
    blocks = [p for p in for_marker if p["verdict"] == "BLOCK"]
    approves = [p for p in for_marker if p["verdict"] == "APPROVE"]

    # INVARIANT: a BLOCK is never downgraded. If a body carries both for this head it is a conflict,
    # and the verdict reported is the BLOCK.
    if blocks:
        return {"present": True, "verdict": "BLOCK", "quoted_sha": blocks[0]["head"],
                "matches_head": True, "conflict": bool(approves), "malformed": bool(problems),
                "detail": ("the body says BOTH BLOCK and APPROVE for this head - resolve it, the PR "
                           "cannot be read as approved" if approves else "BLOCK for this head")}
    if approves:
        return {"present": True, "verdict": "APPROVE", "quoted_sha": approves[0]["head"],
                "matches_head": True, "conflict": False, "malformed": bool(problems),
                "detail": f"APPROVE for this head ({approves[0]['head'][:12]})"}

    # A valid marker, but for a different head. That is not a pass.
    other = parsed[0]
    return {"present": True, "verdict": other["verdict"], "quoted_sha": other["head"],
            "matches_head": False, "conflict": False, "malformed": bool(problems),
            "detail": f"marker names {other['head'][:12]}, which is NOT this head ({head[:12]})"}


def adversary_from_doc(doc: dict, head: str) -> dict:
    """Extract the adversary verdict from an ALREADY-FETCHED `pulls/{pr}` document.

    No API call: the poll keeps the document it fetches, so the verdict is always judged from the
    CURRENT body rather than a startup snapshot (a body edited from APPROVE to BLOCK during the wait
    must be seen).
    """
    labels = {l.get("name") for l in (doc.get("labels") or []) if isinstance(l, dict)}
    out = adversary_verdict(doc.get("body") or "", head)
    out["required_by_label"] = "adversary-required" in labels
    return out


def fetch_checks(repo: str, sha: str) -> list[dict]:
    runs = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}/check-runs?per_page=100", "check_runs")
    statuses = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}/status?per_page=100", "statuses")
    out = []
    for r in runs:
        done = r.get("status") == "completed"
        out.append({"name": r.get("name"), "kind": "check-run", "state": "finished" if done else "pending",
                    "conclusion": r.get("conclusion") if done else None})
    for s in statuses:
        st = s.get("state")
        out.append({"name": s.get("context"), "kind": "status",
                    "state": "pending" if st == "pending" else "finished",
                    "conclusion": None if st == "pending" else st})
    return out


def ci_counts(checks: list[dict]) -> dict:
    pending = sum(1 for c in checks if c["state"] == "pending")
    passed = sum(1 for c in checks if c["state"] == "finished" and c["conclusion"] in PASS_CONCLUSIONS)
    succeeded = sum(1 for c in checks if c["state"] == "finished" and c["conclusion"] == "success")
    return {"total": len(checks), "pass": passed, "success": succeeded,
            "fail": len(checks) - pending - passed, "pending": pending}


# ---- Codex ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_state(body: str) -> dict | None:
    m = STATE_RE.search(body)
    if not m:
        return None
    try:
        doc, _ = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(body, m.end())
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else None


def is_codex_user(c: dict) -> bool:
    """Authored by the Codex app's bot account; anyone can post a comment with Codex's markers."""
    u = c.get("user")
    return isinstance(u, dict) and u.get("login") == CODEX_LOGIN and u.get("type") == "Bot"


def is_codex_app_comment(c: dict) -> bool:
    app = c.get("performed_via_github_app")
    return is_codex_user(c) and isinstance(app, dict) and app.get("slug") == CODEX_APP


def codex_state_for(comments: list[dict], head: str) -> tuple[dict | None, dict | None]:
    """(marker whose code_review.commit_sha is head, newest marker of any head), Codex app comments only."""
    marked = []
    for c in comments:
        body = c.get("body") or ""
        if SUMMARY_MARK in body and is_codex_app_comment(c):
            st = parse_state(body)
            if st is not None:
                marked.append((c.get("updated_at") or c.get("created_at") or "", st))
    marked.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])
    newest = marked[-1][1] if marked else None
    match = None
    for _, st in marked:
        cr = st.get("code_review")
        if isinstance(cr, dict) and cr.get("commit_sha") == head:
            match = st
    return match, newest


def code_review(state: dict | None) -> dict:
    cr = (state or {}).get("code_review")
    return cr if isinstance(cr, dict) else {}


def security_review(state: dict | None) -> dict | None:
    """The marker's `security_review` object, or None when the marker carries none."""
    sr = (state or {}).get("security_review")
    return sr if isinstance(sr, dict) else None


def review_terminal(state: dict | None, head: str) -> bool:
    """Codex is done with the head: the code review is terminal, and so is the security review when the
    marker carries one (a security finding is posted by that review, after or beside the code one)."""
    if code_review(state).get("status") not in TERMINAL:
        return False
    sr = security_review(state)
    return sr is None or (sr.get("status") in TERMINAL and sr.get("commit_sha") in (None, head))


def parse_ts(s: str) -> datetime:
    d = datetime.fromisoformat(s.strip().replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    return d if d.tzinfo else d.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)


THREADS_QUERY = (
    "query($owner:String!,$name:String!,$number:Int!,$cursor:String){repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){"
    "pullRequest(number:$number){reviewThreads(first:100,after:$cursor){pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor} "
    "nodes{isResolved isOutdated comments(first:100){nodes{databaseId author{login} path originalCommit{oid}}}}}}}}"
)


def finding_kind(body: str) -> str | None:
    for mark, kind in FINDING_MARKS:
        if mark in body:
            return kind
    return None


def fetch_threads(repo: str, pr: int) -> list[dict]:
    """Every review thread of the PR: all its comments (databaseId, author, path, original commit; up to
    100 per thread) and whether the thread is resolved / outdated. GraphQL `reviewThreads.isResolved` is the
    authority on whether a finding is still open (PR 32, 2026-10-23: an earlier head's P1 stayed open past
    two pushes). Owner and name go through `-f` (strings: `-F` would turn a digits-only name into an integer)."""
    owner, name = repo.split("/", 1)
    threads: list[dict] = []
    cursor: str | None = None
    seen: set[str] = set()
    while True:
        args = ["api", "graphql", "-f", f"owner={owner}", "-f", f"name={name}", "-F", f"number={pr}",
                "-f", f"query={THREADS_QUERY}"]
        if cursor is not None:
            args += ["-f", f"cursor={cursor}"]
        try:
            doc = json.loads(gh(*args))
            rt = doc["data"]["repository"]["pullRequest"]["reviewThreads"]
            nodes, info = rt["nodes"], rt["pageInfo"]
        except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            raise GhError(f"graphql reviewThreads: unusable answer: {e}") from e
        if doc.get("errors"):
            raise GhError(f"graphql reviewThreads: errors: {str(doc['errors'])[:200]}")
        for n in nodes:
            comments = [{"databaseId": c.get("databaseId"), "author": (c.get("author") or {}).get("login"),
                         "path": c.get("path"), "original_commit_id": (c.get("originalCommit") or {}).get("oid")}
                        for c in ((n.get("comments") or {}).get("nodes") or []) if isinstance(c, dict)]
            threads.append({"comments": comments, "isResolved": bool(n.get("isResolved")),
                            "isOutdated": bool(n.get("isOutdated"))})
        if not info.get("hasNextPage"):
            return threads
        cursor = info.get("endCursor")
        if not cursor or cursor in seen:
            raise GhError("graphql reviewThreads: hasNextPage without a new endCursor")
        seen.add(cursor)


def fetch_review_state(repo: str, pr: int, head: str, since: str | None) -> dict:
    """Codex's findings, code and security alike: `for_head` (selected by head and author, never by a time
    window) and `open`, each entry tagged `kind` (`code` or `security`, by its mark).

    A comment belongs to the head when its `original_commit_id` is the head or its review
    (`pull_request_review_id`) is a `pulls/N/reviews` entry whose `commit_id` is the head: GitHub
    re-anchors `commit_id` of a finding on an unchanged line, but not these two (PR 12, 2026-10-20).
    A later Codex receipt or a quoted `@codex review` therefore cannot hide a finding.

    `open` is every unresolved Codex review thread of ANY head (outdated or not) in which ANY comment is a
    Codex finding (REST: Codex bot, a finding mark; GraphQL: author the Codex app, same databaseId).
    `finding_comments` counts all Codex finding comments per kind, for the marker cross-check."""
    t0 = parse_ts(since) if since else None
    reviews = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/reviews?per_page=100")
    head_reviews = {r.get("id") for r in reviews if r.get("commit_id") == head}
    findings = {}
    for c in api_items(f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments?per_page=100"):
        kind = finding_kind(c.get("body") or "")
        if kind and is_codex_user(c):
            findings[c.get("id")] = (kind, c)
    for_head = []
    for kind, c in findings.values():
        if c.get("original_commit_id") != head and c.get("pull_request_review_id") not in head_reviews:
            continue
        if t0 is not None and not (c.get("created_at") and parse_ts(c["created_at"]) >= t0):
            continue
        for_head.append({"id": c.get("id"), "kind": kind, "path": c.get("path"),
                         "line": c.get("line") or c.get("original_line"),
                         "created_at": c.get("created_at"), "original_commit_id": c.get("original_commit_id"),
                         "body": (c.get("body") or "")[:600]})
    open_threads = []
    for t in fetch_threads(repo, pr):
        if t["isResolved"]:
            continue
        hit = next((x for x in t["comments"] if x["author"] == CODEX_GQL_LOGIN and x["databaseId"] in findings), None)
        if hit is None:
            continue
        kind, c = findings[hit["databaseId"]]
        body = (c.get("body") or "").strip()
        open_threads.append({"id": hit["databaseId"], "kind": kind, "path": hit["path"] or c.get("path"),
                             "line": c.get("line") or c.get("original_line"),
                             "original_commit_id": hit["original_commit_id"] or c.get("original_commit_id"),
                             "isOutdated": t["isOutdated"],
                             "title": (body.splitlines() or [""])[0][:200]})
    return {"for_head": for_head, "open": open_threads,
            "finding_comments": {k: sum(1 for kind, _ in findings.values() if kind == k) for k in KINDS}}


def assess(no_codex: bool, ci_state: str, counts: dict, codex_status: str, cr: dict, sr: dict | None,
           head: str, review: dict | None, adversary: dict | None = None,
           adversary_required: bool = False) -> dict:
    """The findings and merge_ready fields of the summary.

    Both Codex reviews are judged the same way: the code review (`code_review`, mark
    `codex-code-review-finding:v1`) and the security review (`security_review`, mark
    `codex-security-review-finding:v1`). Mismatch (the marker and the threads disagree, so never a clean "0"),
    per kind: the marker says `has_unresolved_findings: true` and no open Codex thread of that kind was
    found, or `false` and some was; or `has_any_findings: true` and no Codex finding comment of that kind
    exists at all. merge_ready needs: at least one CI check that really succeeded, none failing or pending
    (skipped and neutral checks count as passed beside it, never alone); Codex's code review completed on
    this head; its security review completed on this head too WHEN the marker carries `security_review`
    (a marker without one is not blocked on it: the open threads of both kinds still gate, and the summary
    says `security_status: absent`); zero open Codex threads of either kind (outdated or not); no mismatch;
    and a marker that states has_unresolved_findings as a boolean for each review it carries.
    merge_blockers names why not: `codex-<status>` (code review not completed on the head), `security-<status>`
    (`security-failed`, `security-running`, `security-other-head`), `findings-not-collected`,
    `marker-unverified`, `security-marker-unverified`, `no-checks`, `ci-pending`, `ci-failed`,
    `ci-none-succeeded`, `open-findings`, `findings-mismatch`, `codex-skipped`, plus - ONLY when the
    adversary gate is switched on (`--adversary-required` or an `adversary-required` label), or when a
    fetch failed or the marker is malformed - `adversary-missing`, `adversary-stale-or-blocked`,
    `adversary-fetch-failed` or `adversary-malformed`.

    The adversary verdict (added 2026-10-25) is read from a MACHINE-READABLE MARKER in the PR body,
    never from prose: `<!-- adversary-verdict:v1 {"verdict":"APPROVE"|"BLOCK","head":"<40-hex>"} -->`.
    A verdict counts only if the marker is well formed, names exactly APPROVE or BLOCK, and names this
    head. `malformed` is its own outcome and is NEVER ok, even when a valid APPROVE sits beside it; a
    BLOCK is never downgraded by anything. Prose verdicts are not read at all - the first version parsed
    English and three adversary rounds found false approvals and false misses, so that parser was
    deleted rather than patched.
    """
    mismatch: list[str] = []
    blockers: list[str] = []
    for_head = open_n = outdated_n = None
    by_kind_open: dict | None = None
    by_kind_head: dict | None = None
    reviews = [("code", cr)]
    if sr is not None:
        reviews.append(("security", sr))
    if review is not None:
        for_head, open_n = len(review["for_head"]), len(review["open"])
        outdated_n = sum(1 for t in review["open"] if t["isOutdated"])
        by_kind_open = {k: sum(1 for t in review["open"] if t["kind"] == k) for k in KINDS}
        by_kind_head = {k: sum(1 for t in review["for_head"] if t["kind"] == k) for k in KINDS}
        for kind, r in reviews:
            has_any, has_unres = r.get("has_any_findings"), r.get("has_unresolved_findings")
            if has_unres is True and by_kind_open[kind] == 0:
                mismatch.append(f"{kind}: marker has_unresolved_findings=true but no open Codex thread found")
            if has_unres is False and by_kind_open[kind] > 0:
                mismatch.append(f"{kind}: marker has_unresolved_findings=false but "
                                f"{by_kind_open[kind]} open Codex thread(s) found")
            if has_any is True and review["finding_comments"][kind] == 0:
                mismatch.append(f"{kind}: marker has_any_findings=true but no Codex finding comment found")
    if no_codex:
        blockers.append("codex-skipped")
    else:
        if codex_status != "completed":
            blockers.append(f"codex-{codex_status}")
        if sr is not None:
            sstatus = sr.get("status") or "unknown"
            if sr.get("commit_sha") not in (None, head):
                blockers.append("security-other-head")
            elif sstatus != "completed":
                blockers.append(f"security-{sstatus}")
        if review is None:
            blockers.append("findings-not-collected")
        else:
            if not isinstance(cr.get("has_unresolved_findings"), bool):
                blockers.append("marker-unverified")
            if sr is not None and not isinstance(sr.get("has_unresolved_findings"), bool):
                blockers.append("security-marker-unverified")
    if ci_state == "no-checks":
        blockers.append("no-checks")
    elif ci_state != "done":
        blockers.append("ci-pending")
    if counts["fail"]:
        blockers.append("ci-failed")
    elif ci_state == "done" and counts["total"] and not counts["success"]:
        blockers.append("ci-none-succeeded")
    if open_n:
        blockers.append("open-findings")
    if mismatch:
        blockers.append("findings-mismatch")
    # The adversary verdict: REPORTED always, BLOCKING only when opted in. It must not go into
    # `blockers` by default, because `merge_ready = not blockers` and a global default would block
    # every PR in the house - including the operator's own small ones.
    #
    # A FETCH FAILURE is its own outcome, never "missing": reporting an unknown as absent is how a
    # missing gate reads as a passing one (adversary review, F5). And it blocks whenever the gate
    # MIGHT apply - `adversary_required`, or a `required_by_label` we could not read - because a
    # label-gated PR whose body we cannot fetch would otherwise reach `merge_ready: true` with no
    # verdict at all. Fail closed on an unknown.
    adversary_warnings: list[str] = []
    if adversary is not None:
        fetch_failed = bool(adversary.get("fetch_failed"))
        malformed = bool(adversary.get("malformed"))
        # A MALFORMED result is never ok, even when a valid APPROVE sits beside it: a typo in a BLOCK
        # must not let the PR through (adversary review, A1). And `malformed` is its OWN warning, so a
        # typo does not read as an absence (A4).
        ok = (not fetch_failed and not malformed and bool(adversary.get("matches_head"))
              and not str(adversary.get("verdict") or "").upper().startswith("BLOCK"))
        if not ok:
            if fetch_failed:
                adversary_warnings.append("adversary-fetch-failed")
            elif malformed:
                adversary_warnings.append("adversary-malformed")
            elif not adversary.get("present"):
                adversary_warnings.append("adversary-missing")
            else:
                adversary_warnings.append("adversary-stale-or-blocked")
            if adversary_required or fetch_failed or malformed:
                blockers.append(adversary_warnings[-1])
    return {"has_any_findings": cr.get("has_any_findings"), "has_unresolved_findings": cr.get("has_unresolved_findings"),
            "security_status": "absent" if sr is None else (sr.get("status") or "unknown"),
            "security_has_any_findings": None if sr is None else sr.get("has_any_findings"),
            "security_has_unresolved_findings": None if sr is None else sr.get("has_unresolved_findings"),
            "findings_for_head": for_head, "findings_for_head_by_kind": by_kind_head,
            "open_findings": open_n, "open_findings_by_kind": by_kind_open, "open_findings_outdated": outdated_n,
            "findings_mismatch": mismatch, "merge_ready": not blockers, "merge_blockers": blockers,
            # Reported whether or not it blocks, so a PR that should carry a verdict shows up even
            # where blocking is not switched on. `adversary` is the raw finding for the PR body.
            "adversary": adversary, "adversary_warnings": adversary_warnings,
            "adversary_required": adversary_required}


# ---- files ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def write_atomic(path: str, doc: object) -> None:
    tmp = f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(doc, f, indent=1)
    try:
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError:
        Path(tmp).unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise


# ---- requesting the Codex review ------------------------------------------------------------------


def stamp(s: object) -> datetime | None:
    """A server timestamp as an aware UTC datetime; None when absent or unparsable."""
    if not isinstance(s, str):
        return None
    try:
        return parse_ts(s)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def push_stamp(suites: list, timeline: list, head: str) -> datetime | None:
    """When the head was pushed, from SERVER stamps only: the earliest `created_at` of its check suites, and for a
    force-push the `created_at` of the latest `head_ref_force_pushed` item naming the head; the LATEST of the
    stamps that exist. None = no server stamp at all. A commit's own date and its timeline position are never
    used: GitHub places a `committed` item by the commit's author-controlled date."""
    suite_at = [t for t in (stamp(x.get("created_at")) for x in suites if isinstance(x, dict)) if t]
    forced_at = [t for t in (stamp(e.get("created_at")) for e in timeline
                             if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("event") == "head_ref_force_pushed"
                             and isinstance(e.get("commit_id"), str) and e["commit_id"].lower() == head) if t]
    stamps = ([min(suite_at)] if suite_at else []) + ([max(forced_at)] if forced_at else [])
    return max(stamps) if stamps else None


def request_items(timeline: list[dict], allowed: set[str]) -> list[tuple[datetime, int | None]]:
    """(server `created_at`, comment id or None) of every request in the timeline: a `commented` item whose stripped
    body is exactly REQUEST_BODY, whose author is in `allowed` (lower-case logins) and whose `created_at` parses.
    Codex's receipts and other people's quotes of the phrase are never in `allowed`."""
    out = []
    for e in timeline:
        if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get("event") != "commented":
            continue
        who = e.get("user") or e.get("actor")
        login = who.get("login") if isinstance(who, dict) else None
        at = stamp(e.get("created_at"))
        if (isinstance(login, str) and login.lower() in allowed and at is not None
                and isinstance(e.get("body"), str) and e["body"].strip() == REQUEST_BODY):
            cid = e.get("id")
            out.append((at, cid if isinstance(cid, int) and not isinstance(cid, bool) else None))
    return out


def request_times(timeline: list[dict], allowed: set[str]) -> list[datetime]:
    """Server `created_at` of every request (see request_items)."""
    return [at for at, _ in request_items(timeline, allowed)]


def requests_since_push(timeline: list[dict], allowed: set[str], pushed: datetime | None) -> int:
    """Number of `@codex review` requests posted after the head was pushed (`pushed` from push_stamp).

    Requests are those of request_times, strictly later than `pushed` (datetimes, not strings). `pushed` None (no
    server stamp) counts none: the caller leans toward posting, and the cap of two POST attempts per head bounds
    the duplicates. A comment without a parsable `created_at` cannot be shown to follow the push: not counted."""
    if pushed is None:
        return 0
    return sum(1 for at in request_times(timeline, allowed) if at > pushed)


def landed_requests(timeline: list[dict], allowed: set[str], attempt_at: float, pushed: datetime | None,
                    known: set[int]) -> list[int | None]:
    """Ids (None for an item without one) of the requests that a POST attempt made at local epoch `attempt_at` may
    have produced: server `created_at` within [attempt - CLOCK_SKEW, attempt + GH_TIMEOUT + CLOCK_SKEW] (the local
    clock may differ from the server's; a landed POST is created while its gh call runs, so a LATER request is a
    person's, never the attempt's), STRICTLY later than the push stamp when one exists (a request for the previous
    head is never this head's, whatever the skew: floor = max(push, attempt - skew)), and not an id already
    adopted or posted. Needs no push stamp: it is how an errored POST that landed is recognised on a repo
    without check suites; with none, a request for the previous head made within CLOCK_SKEW before the attempt is
    indistinguishable from it (a missed post, bounded by --codex-stuck)."""
    lo, hi = attempt_at - CLOCK_SKEW, attempt_at + GH_TIMEOUT + CLOCK_SKEW
    return [cid for at, cid in request_items(timeline, allowed)
            if lo <= at.timestamp() <= hi and (pushed is None or at > pushed) and (cid is None or cid not in known)]


def finite(x: object) -> float | None:
    """`x` as a finite float, else None (JSON accepts NaN and Infinity; bool is not a number here)."""
    if isinstance(x, bool) or not isinstance(x, (int, float)):
        return None
    try:
        v = float(x)
    except OverflowError:
        return None
    return v if math.isfinite(v) else None


def clamp_count(x: object) -> int:
    """A stored count as an int in 0..MAX_REQUESTS; anything that is not a plain int (bool included) is 0."""
    return max(0, min(x, MAX_REQUESTS)) if isinstance(x, int) and not isinstance(x, bool) else 0


def clamp_ids(x: object) -> list[int]:
    """A stored id list as at most MAX_IDS plain ints (the newest kept); anything else is dropped."""
    return [i for i in x if isinstance(i, int) and not isinstance(i, bool)][-MAX_IDS:] if isinstance(x, list) else []


def load_request_state(path: Path) -> dict:
    """pr-wait's own requests for one head: {"own": 0..MAX_REQUESTS, "attempts": own..MAX_REQUESTS (POSTs tried,
    an errored one included), "last_at": epoch seconds or None, "attempt_at": epoch seconds of a POST that may not
    have been recorded, or None, "ids": [...], "adopted_ids": [int, ...]: ids of the timeline comments already
    counted as pr-wait's own, adopted or posted, at most MAX_IDS}. Every field is read with `.get`; the counts are
    clamped to 0..MAX_REQUESTS (`attempts` never below `own`, so a record from before the field keeps its cap) and a
    non-finite time is dropped; an unreadable file counts as none (the timeline still guards a first post)."""
    try:
        doc = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        doc = None
    doc = doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else {}
    own, ids = clamp_count(doc.get("own")), doc.get("ids")
    return {"own": own, "attempts": max(own, clamp_count(doc.get("attempts"))),
            "last_at": finite(doc.get("last_at")), "attempt_at": finite(doc.get("attempt_at")),
            "ids": ids if isinstance(ids, list) else [], "adopted_ids": clamp_ids(doc.get("adopted_ids"))}


def fetch_login(endpoint: str, *path: str) -> str | None:
    """A login from a JSON document: `path` keys down from the root; None when absent."""
    try:
        node = json.loads(gh("api", endpoint))
    except ValueError as e:
        raise GhError(f"{endpoint}: unparsable answer: {e}") from e
    for k in path:
        node = node.get(k) if isinstance(node, dict) else None
    return node if isinstance(node, str) and node else None


def post_request(repo: str, pr: int) -> dict:
    """Post the one `@codex review` comment (argv list; the body goes through -f, a raw string)."""
    try:
        doc = json.loads(gh("api", "-X", "POST", f"repos/{repo}/issues/{pr}/comments", "-f", f"body={REQUEST_BODY}"))
    except ValueError as e:
        raise GhError(f"posting the request: unparsable answer: {e}") from e
    return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else {}


# ---- duplicate guard ------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_proc_stat(raw: str) -> str | None:
    """starttime (field 22, jiffies since boot) of a /proc/<pid>/stat line; the comm field may hold ')'."""
    try:
        return "proc:" + raw.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[19]
    except IndexError:
        return None


def proc_start(pid: int) -> str | None:
    """An identity for the process that survives pid reuse; None when the pid is gone.

    Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat starttime (jiffies since boot, so a wall-clock step cannot change it).
    Elsewhere (macOS has no /proc): `ps -o lstart=`, which is stored absolutely there."""
    try:
        return parse_proc_stat(Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        if Path("/proc/self/stat").exists():
            return None  # Linux and the pid is gone
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)], check=False, capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=10, env={"LC_ALL": "C", "PATH": os.environ.get("PATH", "")})
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    out = " ".join(p.stdout.split())
    return out if p.returncode == 0 and out else None


def lock_live(doc: object) -> bool:
    if not isinstance(doc, dict) or not isinstance(doc.get("pid"), int) or not doc.get("start"):
        return False
    return proc_start(doc["pid"]) == doc["start"]


def read_lock(path: Path) -> object:
    try:
        return json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


class LockBusy(Exception):
    pass


def create_exclusive(path: Path, doc: dict) -> bool:
    """Create `path` already holding `doc`: write a temp file beside it, then os.link (EEXIST = held).

    O_EXCL then write would leave an empty file a second starter could read as stale."""
    tmp = Path(f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}.{time.monotonic_ns()}")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc))
    try:
        os.link(tmp, path)
        return True
    except FileExistsError:
        return False
    finally:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def acquire_lock(path: Path, info: dict) -> dict | None:
    """Take the lock. Returns None when taken, else the LIVE holder's lock doc.

    A stale lock is taken over under a guard file (`<lock>.takeover`, holding the taker's pid and start,
    broken by liveness, not age) so two takers cannot unlink each other's fresh lock. Residual window:
    two breakers of one orphaned guard can race; the lock itself is still created atomically."""
    guard = Path(f"{path}.takeover")
    ginfo = {"pid": info["pid"], "start": info["start"]}
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 30
    while True:
        if create_exclusive(path, info):
            return None
        doc = read_lock(path)
        if lock_live(doc):
            return doc  # type: ignore[return-value]
        if not create_exclusive(guard, ginfo):
            if not lock_live(read_lock(guard)):
                guard.unlink(missing_ok=True)  # its taker died inside the guard
                continue
            if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                raise LockBusy(f"the lock takeover guard {guard} is held by a live pid for 30 s")
            time.sleep(0.05)
            continue
        try:
            drop_if_stale(path)
        finally:
            gdoc = read_lock(guard)
            if isinstance(gdoc, dict) and gdoc.get("pid") == info["pid"]:
                guard.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def drop_if_stale(path: Path) -> None:
    """Unlink the lock only if a file is there and is not a live waiter's.

    Called inside the takeover guard. A lock that is MISSING must stay missing: another taker may have
    just created a fresh one in the window between our stale read and this reread (adversary round 2, L1);
    an existing but unparseable lock is stale (the writer never leaves one)."""
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    try:
        doc = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        doc = None
    if not lock_live(doc):
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def release_lock(path: Path, pid: int) -> None:
    doc = read_lock(path)
    if isinstance(doc, dict) and doc.get("pid") == pid:
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)


# ---- the wait -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def number(s: str) -> float:
    try:
        v = float(s)
    except ValueError:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f"needs a number of seconds, got '{s}'") from None
    if v < 0:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f"must not be negative, got '{s}'")
    return v


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message: str) -> None:  # argparse exits 2 already; keep the usage text short
        self.exit(EXIT_USAGE, f"pr-wait: {message}\n")


def build_parser() -> Parser:
    p = Parser(prog="pr-wait", description="ONE waiter per PR head: CI checks and Codex's review. "
               "Exit: 0 done, 1 timeout, 2 usage/no such PR, 3 Codex stuck, 4 duplicate waiter alive, "
               "5 head moved, 6 timeout while gh was failing.")
    p.add_argument("pr", type=int, help="pull request number")
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=DEFAULT_REPO, help=f"OWNER/NAME (default {DEFAULT_REPO})")
    p.add_argument("--head", help="head SHA to wait on (default: the PR's current head)")
    p.add_argument("--since", help="optional extra lower bound (ISO time) on a finding's created_at; findings "
                   "are selected by head, not by time")
    p.add_argument("--out", help="output prefix (default ~/.local/state/pr-wait/<repo>-pr<N>-<head12>)")
    p.add_argument("--timeout", type=number, default=3600, help="seconds before giving up (default 3600)")
    p.add_argument("--interval", type=number, default=30, help="seconds between polls (default 30)")
    p.add_argument("--codex-stuck", type=number, default=2700, help="seconds before a non-terminal Codex "
                   "review is reported stuck (default 2700)")
    p.add_argument("--ci-grace", type=number, default=120, help="seconds a head with no checks yet counts as "
                   "pending (default 120)")
    p.add_argument("--no-codex", action="store_true", help="wait for CI only (never requests a review)")
    p.add_argument("--no-request", action="store_true", help="never post `@codex review`; only wait")
    p.add_argument("--adversary-required", action="store_true",
                   help="make a missing/stale adversary verdict in the PR body a merge BLOCKER "
                        "(default: report it in `adversary_warnings` and do not block; also implied by "
                        "an `adversary-required` label on the PR)")
    p.add_argument("--rerequest-after", type=number, default=600, help="seconds a Codex review may stay "
                   "`running` after pr-wait's own request before it is requested once more (default 600; "
                   "at most two requests per head)")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="print the summary as one JSON line")
    return p


def state_dir() -> Path:
    return Path(os.environ.get("PR_WAIT_STATE_DIR") or Path.home() / ".local/state/pr-wait")


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    a = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+", a.repo):
        return usage(f"--repo must be OWNER/NAME, got '{a.repo}'")
    if a.head is not None and not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-fA-F]{40}|[0-9a-fA-F]{64}", a.head):
        return usage(f"--head must be a full commit SHA (40 hex), got '{a.head}'")
    if a.since is not None:
        try:
            parse_ts(a.since)
        except ValueError:
            return usage(f"--since must be an ISO time, got '{a.since}'")
    if a.pr < 1:
        return usage("PR must be a positive number")

    start = time.monotonic()
    deadline = start + a.timeout
    errors: list[str] = []

    # resolve the head; a gh failure is retried, a missing PR is a usage error
    head = a.head
    pr_document: dict | None = None
    if head is None:
        while True:
            try:
                # ONE call to `pulls/{pr}`: keep the document, because the adversary verdict is read
                # from the same one (its body + labels) rather than fetching the endpoint again.
                pr_document = pr_doc(a.repo, a.pr)
                head = pr_document["head"]["sha"]
                break
            except GhError as e:
                if e.not_found:
                    return usage(f"no such PR: {a.repo}#{a.pr} ({e})")
                errors = [str(e)]
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    print(f"PR {a.pr}: timeout, gh failed all along: {e}")
                    return EXIT_GH
                time.sleep(min(a.interval, max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic())))
    else:
        # `--head` was given, so no head resolution happened - but the PR document is still needed for
        # the adversary verdict. Fetching it here (not only on the resolve path) is the fix for a real
        # regression: with `--head`, `s["pr_doc"]` stayed None, `collect_adversary` recorded
        # `fetch_failed`, and `assess` then reported `merge_ready: false` with a FALSE "gh failed"
        # message on a run where gh had not failed.
        try:
            pr_document = pr_doc(a.repo, a.pr)
        except GhError:
            pr_document = None  # a real failure: `fetch_failed` is then the honest answer
    head = head.lower()

    sd = state_dir()
    sd.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    slug = a.repo.replace("/", "__").lower()  # GitHub slugs are case-insensitive: one lock and state per repo
    out = a.out or str(sd / f"{slug}-pr{a.pr}-{head[:12]}")
    lock = sd / f"lock-{slug}-{a.pr}-{head}"
    me = os.getpid()
    my_start = proc_start(me)
    if my_start is None:
        return usage("cannot read this process's start time (/proc/<pid>/stat on Linux, `ps -o lstart=` elsewhere)")
    try:
        held = acquire_lock(lock, {"pid": me, "start": my_start, "repo": a.repo, "pr": a.pr,
                                   "head": head, "out": out})
    except LockBusy as e:
        return usage(str(e))
    if held is not None:
        print(f"pr-wait: already waiting on {a.repo}#{a.pr} at {head[:12]}: pid {held.get('pid')}, "
              f"output prefix {held.get('out')} (read its files; never re-arm a live waiter)", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DUP
    atexit.register(release_lock, lock, me)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: sys.exit(143))
    Path(out).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

    s = {"checks": None, "codex_match": None, "codex_newest": None, "review": None,
         "ci_done": False, "codex_done": a.no_codex, "since": None, "since_source": None,
         "codex_clock": start, "running_seen": False, "posted": [], "state_note": "", "warnings": [],
         "adversary": None, "pr_doc": pr_document}
    rq_path = sd / f"request-{slug}-{a.pr}-{head}.json"
    rq = load_request_state(rq_path)  # pr-wait's own requests for this head, across runs
    rq_ctx: dict = {"exists": False, "allowed": None}

    def request_note(match: dict | None, result: str) -> str:
        parts = list(s["posted"])
        if rq["own"] and not s["posted"]:
            parts.append(f"{rq['own']} request(s) were posted by an earlier run")
        if s["state_note"]:
            parts.append(s["state_note"])
        parts.extend(s["warnings"])
        if (not a.no_codex and rq["own"] and match is not None and not review_terminal(match, head)
                and result != "done"):
            parts.append(f"Codex stayed {code_review(match).get('status') or 'non-terminal'} after {rq['own']} "
                         f"request(s): a manual request is sometimes stuck 'Running' ({STUCK_ISSUE})")
        return "; ".join(parts) or "none needed or not evaluated"

    def finish(result: str, code: int, new_head: str | None = None) -> int:
        checks = s["checks"] or []
        counts = ci_counts(checks)
        cr = code_review(s["codex_match"])
        newest_cr = code_review(s["codex_newest"])
        if a.no_codex:
            codex = {"status": "skipped"}
        else:
            codex = {"status": cr.get("status") or ("absent" if not newest_cr else "other-head"),
                     "commit_sha": cr.get("commit_sha"), "newest_seen_commit_sha": newest_cr.get("commit_sha")}
        ci_state = "done" if s["ci_done"] else "pending"
        if s["ci_done"] and not checks:
            ci_state = "no-checks"
        judged = assess(a.no_codex, ci_state, counts, codex["status"], cr, security_review(s["codex_match"]),
                        head, s["review"], s["adversary"],
                        adversary_required=(a.adversary_required
                                            or bool((s["adversary"] or {}).get("required_by_label"))))
        summary = {"result": result, "exit": code, "repo": a.repo, "pr": a.pr, "head": head,
                   "new_head": new_head, "ci": {"state": ci_state, **counts}, "codex": codex,
                   **judged, "codex_requested": rq["own"], "codex_request_note": request_note(s["codex_match"], result),
                   "since": s["since"], "since_source": s["since_source"], "errors": errors[-3:],
                   "elapsed_s": round(time.monotonic() - start, 1), "out": out}
        write_atomic(f"{out}-checks.json", checks)
        write_atomic(f"{out}-codex.json", None if a.no_codex else (s["codex_match"] or s["codex_newest"] or {}))
        write_atomic(f"{out}-findings.json", None if s["review"] is None else s["review"]["for_head"])
        write_atomic(f"{out}-open-findings.json", None if s["review"] is None else s["review"]["open"])
        write_atomic(f"{out}-summary.json", summary)
        if a.json:
            print(json.dumps(summary, separators=(",", ":")))
        else:
            parts = [f"PR {a.pr} at {head[:12]}: {result}",
                     f"CI {ci_state} ({counts['pass']} pass, {counts['fail']} fail, {counts['pending']} pending)"]
            if not a.no_codex:
                parts.append(f"codex {codex['status']} (security {judged['security_status']})")
                if rq["own"]:
                    parts.append(f"@codex review requested {rq['own']}x")
            if s["review"] is None:
                parts.append("findings not collected")
            else:
                parts.append(f"findings for the head: {len(s['review']['for_head'])}"
                             + (f" (since {s['since']})" if s["since"] else "")
                             + f"; open Codex findings: {judged['open_findings']}"
                             f" ({judged['open_findings_by_kind']['code']} code, "
                             f"{judged['open_findings_by_kind']['security']} security; "
                             f"{judged['open_findings_outdated']} outdated)")
            if judged["findings_mismatch"]:
                parts.append("FINDINGS-MISMATCH: " + " / ".join(judged["findings_mismatch"]))
            # The adversary verdict, reported whether or not it gates. A missing quote is the fact
            # that went unnoticed on PR 61 (2026-10-25), so it is printed even when it does not block.
            adv = judged.get("adversary")
            if adv is not None:
                if adv.get("matches_head"):
                    v = str(adv.get("verdict") or "").upper()
                    parts.append(f"adversary: {'BLOCK' if v.startswith('BLOCK') else adv.get('verdict')}"
                                 f" at this head")
                else:
                    parts.append(f"adversary: {'FETCH FAILED' if adv.get('fetch_failed') else 'missing'}"
                                 f" - {adv.get('detail')}")
            parts.append("merge_ready: " + ("yes" if judged["merge_ready"]
                                            else "no (" + ", ".join(judged["merge_blockers"]) + ")"))
            if new_head:
                parts.append(f"new head {new_head[:12]}")
            if errors and code in (EXIT_TIMEOUT, EXIT_GH):
                parts.append(f"last gh error: {errors[-1]}")
            print("; ".join(parts))
        return code

    fails: list[str] = []  # gh failures of the current poll

    def attempt(fn, *fargs):  # one gh-backed step: a failure is "unknown", retried on the next poll
        try:
            return fn(*fargs)
        except GhError as e:
            fails.append(str(e))
            errors.append(str(e))
            return None

    issue_comments = f"repos/{a.repo}/issues/{a.pr}/comments?per_page=100"

    def collect_findings() -> bool:
        if a.no_codex:
            return True
        review = attempt(fetch_review_state, a.repo, a.pr, head, a.since)
        if review is None:
            return False
        s["review"] = review
        if a.since:
            s["since"], s["since_source"] = a.since, "--since"
        return True

    def collect_adversary() -> bool:
        """Read the adversary verdict from the `pulls/{pr}` document already in hand. NO extra call.

        The verdict lives in the same document `pr_doc()` reads (`body` + `labels`), so this reuses
        `s["pr_doc"]` rather than hitting the endpoint again. That keeps the call count at one per run
        - which the test harness depends on, because it serves routes by CALL COUNT.

        Deliberately separate from collect_findings: it must still run under --no-codex, because the
        adversary is not Codex and a PR can legitimately want the adversary gate without the Codex one.

        A missing document is recorded as `fetch_failed`, NOT as "no verdict" - reporting an unknown as
        absent is how a missing gate gets read as a passing one.
        """
        doc = s.get("pr_doc")
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            s["adversary"] = {"present": False, "verdict": None, "quoted_sha": None,
                              "matches_head": False, "fetch_failed": True,
                              "detail": "the PR document was not fetched (gh failed)"}
            return True
        s["adversary"] = adversary_from_doc(doc, head)
        return True

    def allowed_authors() -> set:
        """Logins whose `@codex review` counts: the PR author, the repo owner, the authenticated gh user."""
        if rq_ctx["allowed"] is None:
            user = fetch_login("user", "login")
            if not user:
                raise GhError("gh api user: no login")
            names = {a.repo.split("/", 1)[0].lower(), user.lower()}
            author = fetch_login(f"repos/{a.repo}/pulls/{a.pr}", "user", "login")
            if author:
                names.add(author.lower())
            rq_ctx["allowed"] = names
        return rq_ctx["allowed"]

    def save_rq() -> None:
        try:
            write_atomic(str(rq_path), rq)
        except OSError as e:  # a warning, never a crash: the process would exit 1, the timeout code
            msg = f"could not write {rq_path.name} ({e}): a later run may not know of these requests"
            if msg not in s["warnings"]:
                s["warnings"].append(msg)

    def post_one(what: str) -> None:
        # the attempt is counted and on record BEFORE the POST: one that errors (a gh timeout) may still have landed,
        # and neither a later poll nor a restarted run may count it as never made
        rq["attempts"] = min(rq["attempts"] + 1, MAX_REQUESTS)
        rq["attempt_at"] = time.time()
        save_rq()
        doc = post_request(a.repo, a.pr)
        rq["own"] += 1
        rq["last_at"] = time.time()
        rq["attempt_at"] = None
        rq["ids"].append(doc.get("id"))
        remember(doc.get("id"))
        save_rq()
        s["posted"].append(f"{what} (comment {doc.get('id')})")
        s["state_note"] = ""

    def known_ids() -> set[int]:
        return set(rq["adopted_ids"]) | {i for i in rq["ids"] if isinstance(i, int) and not isinstance(i, bool)}

    def remember(cid: int | None) -> None:
        """A timeline comment counted as ours (adopted or posted) is never adopted again."""
        if isinstance(cid, int) and not isinstance(cid, bool) and cid not in rq["adopted_ids"]:
            rq["adopted_ids"] = clamp_ids(rq["adopted_ids"] + [cid])

    def count_requests(attempt_at: float | None) -> tuple[int | None, list, list, str]:
        """(requests since the push, ids of the requests the pending POST attempt may have produced (landed_requests),
        ids of the requests since the push, ""), or (None, [], [], why) when a gh call failed: undecidable,
        retried on the next poll."""
        try:
            suites = api_items(f"repos/{a.repo}/commits/{head}/check-suites?per_page=100", "check_suites")
            timeline = api_items(f"repos/{a.repo}/issues/{a.pr}/timeline?per_page=100")
            allowed = allowed_authors()
            pushed = push_stamp(suites, timeline, head)
            landed = [] if attempt_at is None else landed_requests(timeline, allowed, attempt_at, pushed, known_ids())
            since = [] if pushed is None else [cid for at, cid in request_items(timeline, allowed) if at > pushed]
            return len(since), landed, since, ""
        except GhError as e:
            return None, [], [], str(e)

    def request_step(match: dict | None) -> None:
        """Post `@codex review` when the head has no marker and no request since its push, and once more when
        the marker stays non-terminal past --rerequest-after after pr-wait's own request (module docstring)."""
        if a.no_request:
            s["state_note"] = "requesting disabled (--no-request)"
            return
        if rq["own"] >= MAX_REQUESTS:
            return
        pending = rq["attempt_at"] is not None  # an earlier POST errored: it may have landed
        if rq["attempts"] >= MAX_REQUESTS and not pending:
            if (match is not None and rq["own"] == 1 and rq["last_at"] is not None  # due() would hold: say why not
                    and not review_terminal(match, head) and time.time() - rq["last_at"] >= a.rerequest_after):
                s["state_note"] = f"re-request skipped: {rq['attempts']} POST attempts already (one errored)"
            return  # every POST of the head's cap was tried (an errored one counts): never another
        if match is not None and review_terminal(match, head):
            s["state_note"] = "no request needed: the marker for the head is terminal" if not rq["own"] else ""
            return

        def first() -> bool:
            return match is None and not rq["own"] and not rq_ctx["exists"]

        def due() -> bool:
            return (match is not None and rq["own"] == 1 and rq["last_at"] is not None
                    and time.time() - rq["last_at"] >= a.rerequest_after)

        if not (first() or due() or pending):
            if match is not None and not rq["own"]:
                s["state_note"] = "no request needed: a marker for the head exists"
            return
        n, landed, since, why = count_requests(rq["attempt_at"])
        if n is None:
            s["state_note"] = f"undecidable, nothing posted (retried on the next poll): {why}"
            return
        # the errored POST landed: it is ours, so its re-request is not lost and no second post follows it. Found
        # by `landed` (within the skew window of the attempt, later than the push stamp when there is one, not an id
        # already counted) or, below the cap and only with a push stamp, by `n > own` (a request since the push that
        # pr-wait's own count does not explain: it counts only requests AFTER the push, so an old head's request never
        # matches; it has no upper time bound, so it is not used at the cap, where a later person's request would be
        # taken for ours). Which comment was adopted is saved (adopted_ids): never adopted twice.
        known = known_ids()
        fresh = [i for i in since if i is None or i not in known]
        if pending and (landed or (rq["attempts"] < MAX_REQUESTS and n > rq["own"])):
            remember(landed[0] if landed else (fresh[0] if fresh else None))
            rq["own"] += 1
            rq["last_at"], rq["attempt_at"] = rq["attempt_at"], None
            save_rq()
            s["posted"].append("recorded the request whose POST errored but landed (found in the timeline)")
            s["state_note"] = ""
        if rq["attempts"] >= MAX_REQUESTS:
            s["state_note"] = s["state_note"] or f"{rq['attempts']} POST attempts already (an errored one counts): no more"
            return
        if match is None:
            if rq["own"] or rq_ctx["exists"]:
                return
            if n >= 1:
                rq_ctx["exists"] = True
                s["state_note"] = f"a request since the push already exists ({n}): none posted"
                return
            again = False
        else:
            if not due():
                return
            if max(n, rq["own"]) >= MAX_REQUESTS:
                s["state_note"] = f"{n} requests since the push already: no third one"
                return
            again = True
        try:
            post_one("re-requested after the review stayed running" if again else "posted @codex review")
        except GhError as e:
            s["state_note"] = f"posting the request failed, nothing recorded (re-checked on the next poll): {e}"

    while True:
        fails.clear()
        codex_polled = False
        s["ci_done"], s["codex_done"] = False, a.no_codex  # both re-judged on every poll, never latched
        # Keep the document this poll fetched: the adversary verdict is judged from the CURRENT body
        # and labels, not a startup snapshot. A body edited from APPROVE to BLOCK during the wait must
        # be seen, and an `adversary-required` label added mid-wait must be honoured.
        doc = attempt(pr_doc, a.repo, a.pr)
        cur = None
        if doc is not None:
            try:
                cur = doc["head"]["sha"]
            except (KeyError, TypeError):
                cur = None
            s["pr_doc"] = doc
        if cur is not None and cur.lower() != head:
            return finish("head-moved", EXIT_MOVED, cur)
        if cur is not None:
            checks = attempt(fetch_checks, a.repo, head)
            if checks is not None:
                s["checks"] = checks
                s["ci_done"] = not any(c["state"] == "pending" for c in checks) and bool(
                    checks or time.monotonic() - start >= a.ci_grace
                )
            if not a.no_codex:
                comments = attempt(api_items, issue_comments)
                if comments is not None:
                    codex_polled = True
                    match, newest = codex_state_for(comments, head)
                    s["codex_match"], s["codex_newest"] = match, newest
                    if review_terminal(match, head):
                        s["codex_done"], s["running_seen"] = True, False
                    elif match is not None and not s["running_seen"]:
                        s["running_seen"] = True
                        s["codex_clock"] = time.monotonic()
                    request_step(match)
            # finish only when both hold on this same poll
            if s["ci_done"] and s["codex_done"] and collect_findings():
                collect_adversary()  # a warning, never a gate: its failure must not block finishing
                return finish("done", EXIT_DONE)

        now = time.monotonic()
        if codex_polled and not s["codex_done"] and now - s["codex_clock"] >= a.codex_stuck:
            return finish("codex-stuck", EXIT_STUCK)
        if now >= deadline:
            return finish("timeout", EXIT_GH if fails else EXIT_TIMEOUT)
        time.sleep(min(a.interval, max(0.0, deadline - now)))


def usage(msg: str) -> int:
    print(f"pr-wait: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    return EXIT_USAGE


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
